 by one relatively  well-known artists tamenund enraha wat  day and in it there was evident that  suggested that raha wat dey had  correspond with someone I never heard of  a woman named Lorna Pearson and that  there existed somewhere at an archive in  durban there existed a large  correspondence file of correspondence  tween this man in this woman and I never  heard of her I bet I figured I needed  more of evidence so I traveled to Durban  and I went there what I discovered in  fact was that raha wat dey was  corresponding with his former teacher  and that he had been trained by this  woman not to be an artist but to be an  art teacher in schools reserved for  Africans under what was called Bantu  education who's they the white  minorities attempt to control African  education beginning the 1950s and what I  discovered was that that correspondent  file was one of actually hundreds of  correspondence files that existed  between this woman who was the head art  teacher and principal of the school and  her students many of whom never produced  any art works but all of whom thought of  themselves as artists  and they pursued that quest to be  artists under the Bantu education system  by trying to become art educators  because it was very hard to make it as  an artist but if you get someone to pay  you then you could try and make your way  and have some time to do your own you  must have been thrilled to have  discovered that it was a big moment yeah  it was a big moment it was a very short  trip I was with my wife and my at that  point two year old daughter in German  and we had only plans a few days and  those days became frenetic as of illicit  photocopy and picture taken and then  going back and asking permission to use  all of those pictures and images that  I'd collected because I wasn't sure if I  could get back actually I was able to go  back and have been working through that  archive and that's become a major part  of my project let me ask you this of  that sm