in all art education  gets focused instead on rural areas in  areas that are reserved for African  residents okay so one of the things that  you creasing Lisi is the efforts to  maintain a community of artists become  more fraught because artists are being  trained further and further away from  their homes and then they're found in  themselves more and more isolated so  I've tried to collect correspondence  tried to collect evidence of these  connections that these artists are  trying to maintain okay final question I  know the book is not complete yet but  have you been able to come to any  conclusions I have I'm playing around  with some tentative conclusions um I  have some conclusions in terms of how if  you look at artists you see the history  of 20th century South Africa differently  how the chronology is different how the  question of politics changes at various  moments and those are conclusions I  think will be interested to to  historians and to others the conclusion  i'm most excited about that i'm most i  should also say perhaps worried about is  a reassessment of intellectual life  under apartheid the general consensus  had long been that are in scholarship  that under apartheid no authentic  intellectual life was possible there was  either the intellectual life of  resisting the movement which was a sort  which was of course authentic but was  limited because then when apartheid  ended the question of what would happen  or that political energy was was left  open and then there was the idea that  apartheid because of controlled  education becomes a controlled culture  to some extent that it was limiting any  authentic African intellectual  creativity during this time period what  i've discovered is that that wasn't the  case what i've discovered is that under  the system of Bantu education which was  oppressive which was intellectually  spear  in all sorts of ways there existed  elements of creativity and elements of  freedom that teachers tried to cultivate  and so when looking at art education I  see the ways in which art educators were  convinced that they were inculcating  these values of individual creativity  that were vital to being modern it  modern people in the 20th century not  Africans not South Africans just people  it was part of being human and that they  were reading much wider than apartheid  they're reading John Dewey they were  thinking about all these these really  important ideas but they were doing so  under the aegis of the most perfect  system of racial oppression that had  ever that has ever existed and so that's  caused me to ask new questions about the  potential for understanding apartheid I  mean that is very curious ironic it's  deeply ironic and it's very problematic  because when I've mentioned it to people  people say are you suggesting there is  anything good in Bantu education which  is anathema and I'm not suggesting  there's anything good but I'm suggesting  that people made of the system things  that we haven't yet accounted for and  that we need to account for what people  made of the system because when then we  can actually offer a more fair  assessment I think as apartheid recedes  into the past and is now been 18 years  since the end of apartheid people are  beginning to wonder okay what how else  can we see the 20th century does it have  to be just this this analysis of racism  this analysis of state oppression can we  begin to open new sorts of dialogues and  I think that my project hopefully will  contribute to to that to that overall  project fascinating stuff but look  forward to your book thank you for more  information about professor magaziner  and his work please visit our website at  yale edu slash macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale  you
 

